{
  "slug": "gustar",
  "number": 7,
  "part": 2,
  "level": "A1",
  "title": "Gustar and similar verbs",
  "tagline": "Me gusta, me gustan, a mí también: the verb that works «backwards».",
  "goal": "Say what you and others like with gustar, encantar, doler, interesar and molestar, and agree or disagree with también / tampoco.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "estructura",
      "title": "Gustar works backwards",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "*Gustar* means «to be pleasing». The thing you like is the **subject**, and the person is an indirect object: *Me gusta el café* = Coffee is pleasing to me."
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "me / te / le / nos / les + gusta (one thing, a verb) / gustan (several things)"
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            { "fr": "Me {gusta} el chocolate.", "tr": "I like chocolate." },
            { "fr": "Me {gustan} los tacos.", "tr": "I like tacos.", "note": "plural thing → gustan" },
            { "fr": "Nos {gusta} bailar.", "tr": "We like dancing.", "note": "a verb → gusta" },
            { "fr": "¿Te {gustan} los perros?", "tr": "Do you like dogs?" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "Never «yo gusto el café». The verb agrees with the thing, not with you."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a-mi",
      "title": "A mí, a ella, a Diego",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "*le* and *les* can mean several people, so Spanish often adds **a + person** to clarify or to stress it:"
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            { "fr": "{A Diego} le gusta el fútbol.", "tr": "Diego likes soccer." },
            { "fr": "{A mis padres} les encanta viajar.", "tr": "My parents love to travel." },
            { "fr": "{A mí} me gusta el té; {a ti}, el café.", "tr": "I like tea; you like coffee.", "note": "contrast" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": ["Agree", "Disagree"],
          "rows": [
            ["*Me gusta. — A mí {también}.*", "*Me gusta. — A mí {no}.*"],
            ["*No me gusta. — A mí {tampoco}.*", "*No me gusta. — A mí {sí}.*"]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "otros",
      "title": "Other verbs like gustar",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": ["Verb", "Meaning", "Example"],
          "rows": [
            ["*encantar*", "to love (things)", "*Me {encanta} esta canción.*"],
            ["*doler*", "to hurt", "*Me {duelen} los pies.*"],
            ["*interesar*", "to interest", "*¿Te {interesa} la historia?*"],
            ["*molestar*", "to bother", "*Me {molesta} el ruido.*"],
            ["*faltar*", "to be missing", "*Me {faltan} dos euros.*"],
            ["*quedar*", "to fit, to suit", "*Te {queda} muy bien.*"]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "For people, *gustar* can sound romantic: *Me gustas* = I'm into you. For friends, say *Me cae bien* (I like him / her)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "exercises": [
    {
